{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 tools.py read paper:din --find activation --lines 60\npython3 tools.py read unirank-code:INFNet --start-line 120 --lines 155\npython3 tools.py resolve unirank-benchmark-v2\npython3 tools.py read unirank-benchmark-v2 --start-line 1 --lines 100'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 29,\n  \"next_line\": 89,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"29: 5 Training Techniques\\n30: 5.1 Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n31: 5.2 Data Adaptive Activation Function\\n32: 6 Experiments\\n33: 6.1 Datasets and Experimental Setup\\n34: 6.2 Competitors\\n35: 6.3 Metrics\\n36: 6.4 Result from model comparison on Amazon Dataset and MovieLens Dataset\\n37: 6.5 Performance of regularization\\n38: 6.6 Result from model comparison on Alibaba Dataset\\n39: 6.7 Result from online A/B testing\\n40: 6.8 Visualization of DIN\\n41: 7 Conclusions\\n42: References\\n43: License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\\n44: arXiv:1706.06978v4 [stat.ML] 13 Sep 2018\\n45: Deep Interest Network for Click-Through Rate Prediction\\n46: Conference: KDD’18; ; August 19–23, 2018, London, United Kingdom.CCS: Information systems Display advertisingCCS: Information systems Recommender systems\\n47: Guorui Zhou, Chengru Song, Xiaoqiang Zhu\\n48: Ying Fan, Han Zhu, Xiao Ma, Yanghui Yan, Junqi Jin, Han Li, Kun Gai\\n49: Affiliation: Alibaba Group\\n50: email: guorui.xgr, chengru.scr, xiaoqiang.zxq, zhuhan.zh, fanying.fy, maxiao.ma, yanghui.yyh, junqi.jjq, lihan.hl,jingshi.gk@alibaba-inc.com\\n51: © acmcopyright\\n52: Abstract.\\n53: Click-through rate prediction is an essential task in industrial applications, such as online advertising.\\n54: Recently deep learning based models have been proposed, which follow a similar Embedding&MLP paradigm.\\n55: In these methods large scale sparse input features are first mapped into low dimensional embedding vectors, and then transformed into fixed-length vectors in a group-wise manner, finally concatenated together to fed into a multilayer perceptron (MLP) to learn the nonlinear relations among features.\\n56: In this way, user features are compressed into a fixed-length representation vector, in regardless of what candidate ads are.\\n57: The use of fixed-length vector will be a bottleneck, which brings difficulty for Embedding&MLP methods to capture user’s diverse interests effectively from rich historical behaviors.\\n58: In this paper, we propose a novel model: Deep Interest Network (DIN) which tackles this challenge by designing a local activation unit to adaptively learn the representation of user interests from historical behaviors with respect to a certain ad.\\n59: This representation vector varies over different ads, improving the expressive ability of model greatly.\\n60: Besides, we develop two techniques: mini-batch aware regularization and data adaptive activation function which can help training industrial deep networks with hundreds of millions of parameters.\\n61: Experiments on two public datasets as well as an Alibaba real production dataset with over 2 billion samples demonstrate the effectiveness of proposed approaches, which achieve superior performance compared with state-of-the-art methods.\\n62: DIN now has been successfully deployed in the online display advertising system in Alibaba, serving the main traffic.\\n63: Keywords: Click-Through Rate Prediction, Display Advertising, E-commerce\\n64: 1. Introduction\\n65: In cost-per-click (CPC) advertising system, advertisements are ranked by the eCPM (effective cost per mille), which is the product of the bid price and CTR (click-through rate), and CTR needs to be predicted by the system. Hence, the performance of CTR prediction model has a direct impact on the final revenue and plays a key role in the advertising system. Modeling CTR prediction has received much attention from both research and industry community.\\n66: Recently, inspired by the success of deep learning in computer vision (Huang\\n67: et al., ) and natural language processing (Bahdanau\\n68: et al., 2015), deep learning based methods have been proposed for CTR prediction task (Shan\\n69: et al., ; Covington\\n70: et al., 2016; Zhai\\n71: et al., 2016; et al., 2016a).\\n72: These methods follow a similar Embedding&MLP paradigm: large scale sparse input features are first mapped into low dimensional embedding vectors, and then transformed into fixed-length vectors in a group-wise manner, finally concatenated together to fed into fully connected layers (also known as multilayer perceptron, MLP) to learn the nonlinear relations among features.\\n73: Compared with commonly used logistic regression model (Mcmahan\\n74: et al., 2014), these deep learning methods can reduce a lot of feature engineering jobs and enhance the model capability greatly. For simplicity, we name these methods Embedding&MLP in this paper, which now have become popular on CTR prediction task.\\n75: However, the user representation vector with a limited dimension in Embedding&MLP methods will be a bottleneck to express user’s diverse interests. Take display advertising in e-commerce site as an example. Users might be interested in different kinds of goods simultaneously when visiting the e-commerce site. That is to say, user interests are diverse. When it comes to CTR prediction task, user interests are usually captured from user behavior data.\\n76: Embedding&MLP methods learn the representation of all interests for a certain user by transforming the embedding vectors of user behaviors into a fixed-length vector, which is in an euclidean space where all users’ representation vectors are.\\n77: In other words, diverse interests of the user are compressed into a fixed-length vector, which limits the expressive ability of Embedding&MLP methods.\\n78: To make the representation capable enough for expressing user’s diverse interests, the dimension of the fixed-length vector needs to be largely expanded.\\n79: Unfortunately, it will dramatically enlarge the size of learning parameters and aggravate the risk of overfitting under limited data. Besides, it adds the burden of computation and storage, which may not be tolerated for an industrial online system.\\n80: On the other hand, it is not necessary to compress all the diverse interests of a certain user into the same vector when predicting a candidate ad because only part of user’s interests will influence his/her action (to click or not to click). For example, a female swimmer will click a recommended goggle mostly due to the bought of bathing suit rather than the shoes in her last week’s shopping list.\\n81: Motivated by this, we propose a novel model: Deep Interest Network (DIN), which adaptively calculates the representation vector of user interests by taking into consideration the relevance of historical behaviors given a candidate ad.\\n82: By introducing a local activation unit, DIN pays attentions to the related user interests by soft-searching for relevant parts of historical behaviors and takes a weighted sum pooling to obtain the representation of user interests with respect to the candidate ad.\\n83: Behaviors with higher relevance to the candidate ad get higher activated weights and dominate the representation of user interests.\\n84: We visualize this phenomenon in the experiment section.\\n85: In this way, the representation vector of user interests varies over different ads, which improves the expressive ability of model under limited dimension and enables DIN to better capture user’s diverse interests.\\n86: Training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse features is of great challenge.\\n87: For example, SGD based optimization methods only update those parameters of sparse features appearing in each mini-batch. However, adding with traditional ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization, the computation turns to be unacceptable, which needs to calculate L2-norm over the whole parameters (with size scaling up to billions in our situation) for each mini-batch. In this paper, we develop a novel mini-batch aware regularization where only parameters of non-zero features appearing in each mini-batch participate in the calculation of L2-norm, making the computation acceptable. Besides, we design a data adaptive activation function, which generalizes commonly used PReLU(He\\n88: et al., 2015) by adaptively adjusting the rectified point w.r.t. distribution of inputs and is shown to be helpful for training industrial networks with sparse features.\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-code:INFNet\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/salmon1802/UniRank/blob/90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d/model_zoo/INFNet.py\",\n  \"version\": \"git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Full pinned benchmark source for inspection; not proof of equivalence to the original author method.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"e3750a73af1ceb6b2be4bf9355b70a9ad356985a3c465ef27238ef7e0f28fb4c\",\n  \"total_lines\": 313,\n  \"start_line\": 120,\n  \"next_line\": 275,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"120:         self.reset_parameters()\\n121:         self.model_to_device()\\n122: \\n123:     def forward(self, inputs):\\n124:         batch_dict, item_dict, mask, multi_masks = self.get_inputs(inputs, return_multi_masks=True)\\n125:         batch_size = mask.shape[0]\\n126: \\n127:         item_seq_emb = self.embedding_layer(item_dict, flatten_emb=True)\\n128:         item_seq_emb = item_seq_emb.view(batch_size, -1, self.item_info_dim)\\n129: \\n130:         target_emb = item_seq_emb[:, -1, :]  # B x item_info_dim\\n131:         seq_orgs_raw = item_seq_emb[:, 0:-1, :]  # B x S x item_info_dim\\n132: \\n133:         # --- Group 1: Sequence Tokens ---\\n134:         s_orgs = self.seq_org_proj(seq_orgs_raw)  # B x S x token_dim\\n135:         s_hubs = []\\n136:         for i in range(self.num_tasks):\\n137:             hub_i = self.masked_avg_pooling(self.seq_hub_projs[i](seq_orgs_raw), multi_masks[i])\\n138:             s_hubs.append(hub_i)\\n139:         s_hubs = torch.stack(s_hubs, dim=1)  # B x num_tasks x token_dim\\n140: \\n141:         # --- Group 2: Categorical Tokens ---\\n142:         ns_emb = self.embedding_layer(batch_dict, flatten_emb=False)\\n143:         c_orgs_raw = torch.cat([ns_emb, target_emb.view(batch_size, -1, self.embedding_dim)], dim=1)\\n144:         c_orgs, c_hubs = self.unified_tokenizer_layer(c_orgs_raw)\\n145: \\n146:         # --- Group 3: Task Tokens ---\\n147:         t_orgs = self.task_orgs.expand(batch_size, -1, -1)\\n148:         t_shared = self.task_shared_hubs.expand(batch_size, -1, -1)\\n149: \\n150:         # Update Tokens and Hubs through multi-layer stacked INFNetBlocks\\n151:         t_orgs = self.activation_checkpoint(\\n152:             self.unified_interaction_layers,\\n153:             c_orgs,\\n154:             s_orgs,\\n155:             t_orgs,\\n156:             c_hubs,\\n157:             s_hubs,\\n158:             t_shared,\\n159:             mask\\n160:         )\\n161: \\n162:         # --- Multi-Task Prediction ---\\n163:         tower_output = [self.tower[i](t_orgs[:, i, :]) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n164:         y_pred = [self.output_activation[i](tower_output[i]) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n165: \\n166:         return_dict = {}\\n167:         labels = self.feature_map.labels\\n168:         for i in range(self.num_tasks):\\n169:             return_dict[\\\"{}_pred\\\".format(labels[i])] = y_pred[i]\\n170:         return return_dict\\n171: \\n172: \\n173: class INFNetCategoricalTokenizer(nn.Module):\\n174:     def __init__(self, embedding_dim, token_dim, num_c_hub_token, num_field, expansion_factor=4):\\n175:         super(INFNetCategoricalTokenizer, self).__init__()\\n176:         input_dim = num_field * embedding_dim\\n177:         self.c_hub_mlps = AutoSplitTokenizer(input_dim, token_dim, num_c_hub_token)\\n178:         self.c_org_mlps = FieldWiseTokenizer(embedding_dim, token_dim, num_field)\\n179: \\n180:     def forward(self, c_orgs_raw):\\n181:         B = c_orgs_raw.size(0)\\n182:         c_hubs_flat = c_orgs_raw.view(B, -1)\\n183:         c_orgs = self.c_org_mlps(c_orgs_raw)\\n184:         c_hubs = self.c_hub_mlps(c_hubs_flat)\\n185:         return c_orgs, c_hubs\\n186: \\n187: \\n188: class INFNetBlocks(nn.Module):\\n189:     \\\"\\\"\\\"INFNet core module that manages multi-layer stacking: including Phase 1 (Aggregation) and Phase 2 (Broadcast)\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n190: \\n191:     def __init__(self, num_layers, token_dim, num_c_hubs, num_s_hubs, num_t_hubs, expansion_factor=4):\\n192:         super(INFNetBlocks, self).__init__()\\n193:         self.num_layers = num_layers\\n194: \\n195:         # Use nn.ModuleList to stack multiple layers of Aggregation and Broadcast respectively\\n196:         self.aggregation_layers = nn.ModuleList([\\n197:             Aggregation(token_dim) for _ in range(num_layers)\\n198:         ])\\n199:         self.broadcast_layers = nn.ModuleList([\\n200:             Broadcast(token_dim, num_c_hubs, num_s_hubs, num_t_hubs, expansion_factor)\\n201:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n202:         ])\\n203: \\n204:     def forward(self, c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, c_hubs, s_hubs, t_shared, s_mask=None):\\n205:         # Equation (4): Task Hubs are composed of Specific and Shared\\n206:         t_hubs = torch.cat([t_orgs, t_shared], dim=1)\\n207:         # Iterative update layer by layer\\n208:         for i in range(self.num_layers):\\n209:             # Phase 1: Multi-View Global Aggregation\\n210:             c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs = self.aggregation_layers[i](\\n211:                 c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs, c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, s_mask\\n212:             )\\n213: \\n214:             # Phase 2: Global-to-Local Affine Broadcast\\n215:             c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs = self.broadcast_layers[i](\\n216:                 c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs\\n217:             )\\n218: \\n219:         return t_orgs\\n220: \\n221: \\n222: class Aggregation(nn.Module):\\n223:     \\\"\\\"\\\" Multi-View Global Aggregation\\n224:     Keep SDPA mask-free to preserve FlashAttention eligibility, accepting minor attention leakage.\\n225:     \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n226:     def __init__(self, token_dim):\\n227:         super(Aggregation, self).__init__()\\n228:         # Shared Keys/Values projection to reduce calculations\\n229:         self.k_proj_c = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n230:         self.v_proj_c = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n231:         self.k_proj_s = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n232:         self.v_proj_s = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n233:         self.k_proj_t = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n234:         self.v_proj_t = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n235: \\n236:         # Independent Hub aggregator for each group\\n237:         self.c_agg = HubSpecificAggregator(token_dim)\\n238:         self.s_agg = HubSpecificAggregator(token_dim)\\n239:         self.t_agg = HubSpecificAggregator(token_dim)\\n240: \\n241:     def forward(self, c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs, c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, s_mask=None):\\n242:         K_c, V_c = self.k_proj_c(c_orgs), self.v_proj_c(c_orgs)\\n243:         K_s, V_s = self.k_proj_s(s_orgs), self.v_proj_s(s_orgs)\\n244:         K_t, V_t = self.k_proj_t(t_orgs), self.v_proj_t(t_orgs)\\n245: \\n246:         # Processing sequence Mask broadcast dimensions (B, 1, T)\\n247:         if s_mask is not None:\\n248:             K_s = K_s * s_mask.unsqueeze(-1).float()\\n249:             V_s = V_s * s_mask.unsqueeze(-1).float()\\n250: \\n251:         c_hubs_out = self.c_agg(c_hubs, K_c, V_c, K_s, V_s, K_t, V_t)\\n252:         s_hubs_out = self.s_agg(s_hubs, K_c, V_c, K_s, V_s, K_t, V_t)\\n253:         t_hubs_out = self.t_agg(t_hubs, K_c, V_c, K_s, V_s, K_t, V_t)\\n254: \\n255:         return c_hubs_out, s_hubs_out, t_hubs_out\\n256: \\n257: class HubSpecificAggregator(nn.Module):\\n258:     # Keep SDPA mask-free to preserve FlashAttention eligibility, accepting minor attention leakage.\\n259:     def __init__(self, token_dim):\\n260:         super(HubSpecificAggregator, self).__init__()\\n261:         self.q_proj = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n262:         self.fuse = nn.Linear(token_dim * 3, token_dim)\\n263:         self.norm = nn.LayerNorm(token_dim)\\n264:         self.dot_attention = ScaledDotProductAttention()\\n265: \\n266:     def forward(self, hubs, K_c, V_c, K_s, V_s, K_t, V_t):\\n267:         Q = self.q_proj(hubs)\\n268:         scale = Q.size(-1) ** 0.5\\n269:         Z_c, _ = self.dot_attention(Q, K_c, V_c, scale=scale)\\n270:         Z_s, _ = self.dot_attention(Q, K_s, V_s, scale=scale)\\n271:         Z_t, _ = self.dot_attention(Q, K_t, V_t, scale=scale)\\n272:         Z_fused = self.fuse(torch.cat([Z_c, Z_s, Z_t], dim=-1))\\n273:         return self.norm(hubs + Z_fused)\\n274: \"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"title\": \"UniRank: Benchmarking Ranking Models for Unified Sequential Modeling and Feature Interaction\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2607.19987v2\",\n  \"kind\": \"benchmark_paper\",\n  \"content_read\": true,\n  \"read_scope\": \"Section2.3 representative models; protocol and efficiency checked separately during adapter setup.\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Cached section2.3 method descriptions; not an original-method full-paper audit.\",\n  \"retrieved_on\": \"2026-11-06\",\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_reference\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false,\n  \"text_sha256\": \"34ff9c412b35f3118aae377faca5f658d7bffac0b4ef1c12a4eb0c7937372789\",\n  \"content_sha256\": \"a5a9d5475c2e09fc89caa6a9b4b5d2da0f48aef28fea78549278f6388ebfb5d4\",\n  \"text_ref\": \"source-text://unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"text_lines\": 39\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2607.19987v2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Cached section2.3 method descriptions; not an original-method full-paper audit.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"34ff9c412b35f3118aae377faca5f658d7bffac0b4ef1c12a4eb0c7937372789\",\n  \"total_lines\": 39,\n  \"start_line\": 1,\n  \"next_line\": 40,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"1: Source: https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\\n2: Version: arxiv:2607.19987v2\\n3: Scope: section2.3 only, original benchmark method descriptions.\\n4: \\n5: 2.3. Representative Models\\n6: 2.3.1. Stacked Unified Interaction\\n7: This paradigm arranges sequential modeling and feature interaction as consecutive modules. The sequence module first encodes the behavioral history, and the feature interaction module then combines this representation with user, target-item, and context features.\\n8: •\\n9: HiFormer (Gui et al., 2023) pools the behavior history with target attention, tokenizes heterogeneous fields and task queries, and applies hierarchical HiFormer blocks to the stacked tokens.\\n10: •\\n11: RankMixer (Zhu et al., 2025) pools the history with target attention, tokenizes concatenated ranking fields into chunks, and alternates parameter-free Token Mixing with per-token MLPs.\\n12: •\\n13: Zenith (Zhang et al., 2026) separately embeds user and item identifiers, pools historical item attributes with target attention, tokenizes context and target attributes, and applies Zenith interaction blocks.\\n14: •\\n15: TokenMixer (Jiang et al., 2026) combines target-aware sequence summaries with global and group tokens, then applies reversible token mixing and per-token feed-forward networks.\\n16: •\\n17: UniMixer (Ha et al., 2026) flattens histories with user and context fields, then chunk-tokenizes them for UniMixing-Lite blocks.\\n18: •\\n19: HeMix (Wang et al., 2026) combines fixed and feature-conditioned queries with low-rank token mixing over recent and earlier histories.\\n20: •\\n21: SSR (Yu et al., 2026) uses target-attention sequence pooling and multi-view competitive sparse filters with dense fusion.\\n22: 2.3.2. Layer-wise Unified Interaction\\n23: This paradigm integrates sequential and non-sequential representations throughout every network layer.\\n24: •\\n25: OneTrans (Zhang et al., 2025) injects sequence and partitioned non-sequential tokens into every block, using mixed attention, a shared sequence FFN, and token-specific FFNs.\\n26: •\\n27: HyFormer (Huang et al., 2026b) encodes the sequence per layer, decodes global queries, and enriches them with non-sequential tokens.\\n28: •\\n29: MixFormer (Huang et al., 2026a) mixes non-sequential queries, uses cross-attention to retrieve information from the updated sequence, and fuses the resulting representations.\\n30: •\\n31: INFNet (Li et al., 2025b) organizes categorical, sequential, and task tokens around a hub for layer-wise aggregation and broadcasting.\\n32: •\\n33: EST (Liu et al., 2026) uses cross-attention to sequence representations and feature-specific feed-forward layers.\\n34: •\\n35: TokenFormer (Zhou et al., 2026) represents fields, histories, and targets as tokens with full interaction and sliding-window Flex Attention.\\n36: •\\n37: LONGER (Chai et al., 2025) encodes local groups with inner Transformers and joins compressed long-term history with recent queries.\\n38: •\\n39: UltraHSTU (Ding et al., 2026) extends HSTU-style sequential transduction (Zhai et al., 2024) with hybrid attention that combines sparse and full attention in each interaction layer.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
