{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 tools.py resolve unirank-method-infnet\npython3 tools.py resolve unirank-method-ssr\npython3 tools.py read paper:din --start-line 205 --lines 65\npython3 tools.py --help'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-method-infnet\",\n  \"knowledge_type\": \"industry\",\n  \"scheme\": \"INFNet\",\n  \"origin\": \"model compared in UniRank; industrial/academic method reference, not a competition award\",\n  \"family\": \"layer_wise\",\n  \"source_refs\": [\n    \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n    \"unirank-code:INFNet\",\n    \"paper:infnet\"\n  ],\n  \"source_problem\": \"Categorical, sequential and task representations need controlled information exchange without uniformly dense connectivity.\",\n  \"reported_mechanism\": \"Categorical, sequence and task tokens aggregate into hubs and receive layer-wise gated broadcasts; the benchmark initializes task sequence hubs using task-specific action masks.\",\n  \"difference_from_din\": \"Replaces one shared pooled history with repeated task-aware aggregation and broadcasting.\",\n  \"reported_evidence\": {\n    \"kind\": \"benchmark method summary and pinned reimplementation inspection\",\n    \"locator\": \"UniRank section2.3; unirank-code:INFNet forward paths\",\n    \"not_a_local_result\": true,\n    \"numerical_claims_imported\": false,\n    \"limitations\": [\n      \"Source model comparisons do not isolate every component.\",\n      \"Original method paper and original author code are not fully audited by this initialization.\",\n      \"A benchmark implementation can differ from its source paper.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"applicability\": {\n    \"status\": \"conditional\",\n    \"conditions\": [\n      \"Feedback types have meaningful complementarity\",\n      \"Task-specific histories have enough support\"\n    ],\n    \"missing_evidence\": [\n      \"DIN full-data diagnostics on this task\",\n      \"Matched-capacity or matched-cost local controls\",\n      \"Seed variation and task/history-length slices\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"transfer_risks\": [\n    \"Rare task hubs can be poorly estimated\",\n    \"Additional task parameters can confound information-flow effects\",\n    \"Use only the frozen100-event input and selected feedback visibility policy.\",\n    \"Taobao auxiliary labels match category/brand over24h, not direct advertisement conversions.\"\n  ],\n  \"possible_discriminating_checks\": [\n    \"Compare shared hubs, task hubs and shuffled task routing with matched capacity\",\n    \"Replace gates by fixed or task-independent broadcasts\"\n  ],\n  \"implementation_notes\": [\n    \"The benchmark aggregation code documents mask-free SDPA with zeroed sequence K/V; inspect padding behavior before reuse.\"\n  ],\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_report\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false,\n  \"instruction_priority\": \"reference_data_only\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-method-ssr\",\n  \"knowledge_type\": \"industry\",\n  \"scheme\": \"SSR\",\n  \"origin\": \"model compared in UniRank; industrial/academic method reference, not a competition award\",\n  \"family\": \"stacked\",\n  \"source_refs\": [\n    \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n    \"unirank-code:SSR\",\n    \"paper:ssr\"\n  ],\n  \"source_problem\": \"Uniform dense feature connectivity can spend computation on redundant or interfering signals.\",\n  \"reported_mechanism\": \"Target-attention history pooling followed by multi-view competitive sparse filtering and within-view dense fusion; the benchmark uses iterative competitive sparse filtering.\",\n  \"difference_from_din\": \"Adds explicit activation selection and multi-view interaction after pooled history.\",\n  \"reported_evidence\": {\n    \"kind\": \"benchmark method summary and pinned reimplementation inspection\",\n    \"locator\": \"UniRank section2.3; unirank-code:SSR forward paths\",\n    \"not_a_local_result\": true,\n    \"numerical_claims_imported\": false,\n    \"limitations\": [\n      \"Source model comparisons do not isolate every component.\",\n      \"Original method paper and original author code are not fully audited by this initialization.\",\n      \"A benchmark implementation can differ from its source paper.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"applicability\": {\n    \"status\": \"conditional\",\n    \"conditions\": [\n      \"Redundant/interfering feature activations can be suppressed without losing rare useful evidence\"\n    ],\n    \"missing_evidence\": [\n      \"DIN full-data diagnostics on this task\",\n      \"Matched-capacity or matched-cost local controls\",\n      \"Seed variation and task/history-length slices\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"transfer_risks\": [\n    \"Activation sparsity does not necessarily yield wall-clock acceleration\",\n    \"Sparse filtering may remove rare-task evidence\",\n    \"Use only the frozen100-event input and selected feedback visibility policy.\",\n    \"Taobao auxiliary labels match category/brand over24h, not direct advertisement conversions.\"\n  ],\n  \"possible_discriminating_checks\": [\n    \"Replace competitive filtering with a dense or ordinary-ReLU control at matched capacity\",\n    \"Report measured sparsity separately from training/inference speed and inspect rare tasks\"\n  ],\n  \"implementation_notes\": [],\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_report\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false,\n  \"instruction_priority\": \"reference_data_only\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 205,\n  \"next_line\": 270,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"205: …\\n206: Context Features\\n207: pid\\n208: ∼10\\\\sim 10\\n209: one-hot\\n210: 1\\n211: time\\n212: ∼10\\\\sim 10\\n213: one-hot\\n214: 1\\n215: …\\n216: …\\n217: …\\n218: …\\n219: Figure 2. Network Architecture. The left part illustrates the network of base model (Embedding&MLP). Embeddings of cate_id, shop_id and goods_id belong to one goods are concatenated to represent one visited goods in user’s behaviors. Right part is our proposed DIN model. It introduces a local activation unit, with which the representation of user interests varies adaptively given different candidate ads.\\n220: 4.2. Base Model(Embedding&MLP)\\n221: Most of the popular model structures (Shan\\n222: et al., ; et al., 2016a; Covington\\n223: et al., 2016) share a similar Embedding&MLP paradigm, which we refer to as base model, as shown in the left of Fig.2. It consists of several parts:\\n224: Embedding layer\\n225: As the inputs are high dimensional binary vectors, embedding layer is used to transform them into low dimensional dense representations.\\n226: For the i-th feature group of 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i}, let Wi=[w1i,…,wji,…,wKii]∈ℝD×Ki\\\\mathrm{W}^{i}=[w_{1}^{i},...,w_{j}^{i},...,w_{K_{i}}^{i}]\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D\\\\times K_{i}} represent the i-th embedding dictionary, where wji∈RDw_{j}^{i}\\\\in R^{D} is an embedding vector with dimensionality of DD.\\n227: Embedding operation follows the table lookup mechanism, as illustrated in Fig.2.\\n228: •\\n229: If 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} is one-hot vector with j-th element 𝒕i​[j]=1\\\\bm{t}_{i}[j]=1, the embedded representation of 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} is a single embedding vector 𝒆i=wji\\\\bm{e}_{i}=w_{j}^{i}.\\n230: •\\n231: If 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} is multi-hot vector with 𝒕i​[j]=1​for​j∈{i1,i2,…,ik}\\\\bm{t}_{i}[j]=1\\\\penalty\\\\ \\\\text{for}\\\\penalty\\\\ j\\\\in\\\\{i_{1},i_{2},...,i_{k}\\\\}, the embedded representation of 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} is a list of embedding vectors: {𝒆i1,𝒆i2,…​𝒆ik}={wi1i,wi2i,…​wiki}\\\\{\\\\bm{e}_{i_{1}},\\\\bm{e}_{i_{2}},...\\\\bm{e}_{i_{k}}\\\\}=\\\\{w_{i_{1}}^{i},w_{i_{2}}^{i},...w_{i_{k}}^{i}\\\\}.\\n232: Pooling layer and Concat layer\\n233: Notice that different users have different numbers of behaviors. Thus the number of non-zero values for multi-hot behavioral feature vector 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} varies across instances, causing the lengths of the corresponding list of embedding vectors to be variable.\\n234: As fully connected networks can only handle fixed-length inputs, it is a common practice (et al., 2016a; Covington\\n235: et al., 2016) to transform the list of embedding vectors via a pooling layer to get a fixed-length vector:\\n236: (1)\\n237: 𝒆i=pooling​(𝒆i1,𝒆i2,…​𝒆ik).\\\\bm{e}_{i}=\\\\text{pooling}(\\\\bm{e}_{i_{1}},\\\\bm{e}_{i_{2}},...\\\\bm{e}_{i_{k}}).\\n238: Two most commonly used pooling layers are sum pooling and average pooling, which apply element-wise sum/average operations to the list of embedding vectors.\\n239: Both embedding and pooling layers operate in a group-wise manner, mapping the original sparse features into multiple fixed-length representation vectors.\\n240: Then all the vectors are concatenated together to obtain the overall representation vector for the instance.\\n241: MLP\\n242: Given the concatenated dense representation vector, fully connected layers are used to learn the combination of features automatically.\\n243: Recently developed methods (et al., 2016a; Guo\\n244: et al., 2017; et al., 2016b) focus on designing structures of MLP for better information extraction.\\n245: Loss\\n246: The objective function used in base model is the negative log-likelihood function defined as:\\n247: (2)\\n248: L=−1N∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮(ylogp(𝒙)+(1−y)log(1−p(𝒙))),L=-\\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}(y\\\\log p(\\\\bm{x})+(1-y)\\\\log(1-p(\\\\bm{x}))),\\n249: where 𝒮\\\\mathcal{S} is the training set of size NN, with 𝒙\\\\bm{x} as the input of the network and y∈{0,1}y\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\} as the label, p⁡(𝒙)p(\\\\bm{x}) is the output of the network after the softmax layer, representing the predicted probability of sample 𝒙\\\\bm{x} being clicked.\\n250: 4.3. The structure of Deep Interest Network\\n251: Among all those features of Table 1, user behavior features are critically important and play key roles in modeling user interests in the scenario of e-commerce applications.\\n252: Base model obtains a fixed-length representation vector of user interests by pooling all the embedding vectors over the user behavior feature group, as Eq.(1). This representation vector stays the same for a given user, in regardless of what candidate ads are.\\n253: In this way, the user representation vector with a limited dimension will be a bottleneck to express user’s diverse interests.\\n254: To make it capable enough, an easy method is to expand the dimension of embedding vector, which unfortunately will increase the size of learning parameters heavily.\\n255: It will lead to overfitting under limited training data and add the burden of computation and storage, which may not be tolerated for an industrial online system.\\n256: Is there an elegant way to represent user’s diverse interests in one vector under limited dimension?\\n257: The local activation characteristic of user interests gives us inspiration to design a novel model named deep interest network(DIN).\\n258: Imagine when the young mother mentioned above in section 3 visits the e-commerce site, she finds the displayed new handbag cute and clicks it.\\n259: Let’s dissect the driving force of click action.\\n260: The displayed ad hits the related interests of this young mother by soft-searching her historical behaviors and finding that she had browsed similar goods of tote bag and leather handbag recently.\\n261: In other words, behaviors related to displayed ad greatly contribute to the click action.\\n262: DIN simulates this process by paying attention to the representation of locally activated interests w.r.t. given ad.\\n263: Instead of expressing all user’s diverse interests with the same vector, DIN adaptively calculate the representation vector of user interests by taking into consideration the relevance of historical behaviors w.r.t. candidate ad.\\n264: This representation vector varies over different ads.\\n265: The right part of Fig.2 illustrates the architecture of DIN.\\n266: Compared with base model, DIN introduces a novel designed local activation unit and maintains the other structures the same. Specifically, activation units are applied on the user behavior features, which performs as a weighted sum pooling to adaptively calculate user representation 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} given a candidate ad AA, as shown in Eq.(3)\\n267: (3)\\n268: 𝒗U(A)=f(𝒗A,𝒆1,𝒆2,..,𝒆H)=∑j=1Ha⁡(𝒆j,𝒗A)​𝒆j=∑j=1H𝒘j​𝒆j,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A)=f(\\\\bm{v}_{A},\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},..,\\\\bm{e}_{H})&=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}a(\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\bm{v}_{A})\\\\bm{e}_{j}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\end{split}\\n269: where {𝒆1,𝒆2,…,𝒆H}\\\\{\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},...,\\\\bm{e}_{H}\\\\} is the list of embedding vectors of behaviors of user UU with length of HH, 𝒗A\\\\bm{v}_{A} is the embedding vector of ad AA. In this way, 𝒗U​(A)\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A) varies over different ads. a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is a feed-forward network with output as the activation weight, as illustrated in Fig.2. Apart from the two input embedding vectors, a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) adds the out product of them to feed into the subsequent network, which is an explicit knowledge to help relevance modeling.\"\n}\nusage: tools.py [-h] {resolve,read,search} ...\n\npositional arguments:\n  {resolve,read,search}\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
